<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Results and Discussion</h2>

<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reward functions.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We determine an appropriate reward function in the predictive framework by evaluating oracle routers associated with them. We compare <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> reward functions, the traditionally used linear trade-off (<math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in Eqn. (<a href="#S6.E3" title="In Reward functions. ‣ 6 Results and Discussion ‣ One Head, Many Models: Cross-Attention Routing for Cost-Aware LLM Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>)) and novel exponential trade-off (<math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in Eqn. (<a href="#S6.E3" title="In Reward functions. ‣ 6 Results and Discussion ‣ One Head, Many Models: Cross-Attention Routing for Cost-Aware LLM Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>)), proposing the latter as an appropriate reward function. For a user prompt <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> with an LLM response <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, the performance <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(q,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(q,r)</annotation></semantics></math> and generation cost <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(q,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(q,r)</annotation></semantics></math> are combined in reward functions as:</p>
<table id="S6.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}=s-\frac{1}{\lambda}c,\;\;\;\;R_{2}=s\times\exp\left(-\frac{1}{\lambda}c\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>λ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.287em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>λ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}=s-\frac{1}{\lambda}c,\;\;\;\;R_{2}=s\times\exp\left(-\frac{1}{\lambda}c\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Though both the reward functions have similar AIQ scores (in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Baselines. ‣ 4 Evaluation Methodology ‣ One Head, Many Models: Cross-Attention Routing for Cost-Aware LLM Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) while routing at most <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20\%</annotation></semantics></math> of user prompts to the expensive LLM, the <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>-sensitivity of <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> oracle router is drastically lesser than that of <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> router, indicating stability of <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> reward function over traditional linear trade-off <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. This could be attributed to the boundedness of the exponential trade-off while linear trade-off is unbounded.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cost-efficiency of LLM Routers</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S6.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Ablation: Different Architectures in predictor-based LLM routing framework ‣ 6 Results and Discussion ‣ One Head, Many Models: Cross-Attention Routing for Cost-Aware LLM Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ LLM Blender Implementation. ‣ 5 Implementation Details ‣ One Head, Many Models: Cross-Attention Routing for Cost-Aware LLM Selection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> show the performance of our attention-based router against KNN, SVM, and MLP baselines. The key metric is Average Improvement in Quality (AIQ), defined as the area under the cost–quality Pareto frontier. Across all LLM pools, the attention router achieves higher AIQ. In LLM pool 1, it improves AIQ by at least <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\%</annotation></semantics></math> over baselines. In other pools with similarly performing models, it outperforms KNN and SVM by at least <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="33.58\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>33.58</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">33.58\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="29.41\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>29.41</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">29.41\%</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, along with higher maximum performance (<math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Perf}_{\text{Max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>Perf</mtext><mtext>Max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Perf}_{\text{Max}}</annotation></semantics></math>). These results highlight the advantage of our similarity-based routing objective in both performance and cost-efficiency, and demonstrates that attention-based routing provides a robust and generalizable improvement.</p>
</div>
</section>
